<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Expect My Future to Be... – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Goals</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Expect My Future to Be...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a diary entry about a significant event. Interactive: Collaborate in writing, with guidance, on a shared text for a group project.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a diary entry about future academic goals (e.g., "I have to send my application to the university"). Can collaborate with peers to write about plans for high school, using a structured outline with clear steps and future goals (e.g., "First, we will complete our science classes next, we will graduate with a Bachillerato en Ciencias to apply for university"). Can collaborate on a group project outline for future studies (e.g., "First, I have to research careers, then, I have to make a plan").
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students watch a short video (1-2 minutes) about a teenager discussing their plans after high school (e.g., "I want to go to university," "I plan to travel"). After the video, the teacher shows images related to different career paths (e.g., doctor, engineer, artist) and asks students to briefly think about one they are interested in. Students jot down 1-2 words about their initial thoughts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes on the board, "My future goal is to be a teacher." Then adds, "I <b>have to</b> study at the university. I <b>must</b> prepare my application carefully. In four years, I <b>will be working</b> in a school." The teacher explicitly points out the use of "have to," "must," and "will be working." The teacher also models the pronunciation of future tense structures and modals (e.g., "I will study hard." "I have to be the best student in my class."). The teacher writes simple sentences on the board incorporating vocabulary like <i>future, plan, career, achieve, ambitiously</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I <b>have to</b> send my application,' does it mean it's optional or necessary?" (Expected answer: Necessary)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'In five years, I <b>will be working</b>,' am I talking about now, yesterday, or a continuous action in the future?" (Expected answer: A continuous action in the future)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher checks for understanding of minimal pairs /θ/ in "math" and /s/ in "mass" through quick repetition drills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short blog post or a series of three short diary entries from different 8th-grade students describing their aspirations for high school and beyond, using modals of necessity, WH-questions, future continuous, and target vocabulary (e.g., <i>future, goals, plan, career, opportunity, achieve, ambitious</i>). The text includes phrases like, "I <b>have to</b> choose my electives soon," "What <b>are your plans</b> after graduation?", "I <b>will be studying</b> science."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret short, simple written texts about personal interests and future plans, identifying key information about goals and necessary steps.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main idea of these diary entries?" (To share future plans/goals). "Are these students talking about the past or the future?" (The future).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match each diary entry (A, B, C) to a specific future goal (e.g., going to university, starting a business, becoming an engineer).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements about the diary entries and mark them as True or False (e.g., "One student must travel for his future career." "Another student plans to achieve ambitious goals.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences and short paragraphs using modals of necessity ("have to," "must"), WH-questions for educational and career goals, future continuous, and target vocabulary (nouns: <i>future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development</i>; verbs: <i>plan, achieve, prepare, set, have to</i>; adjectives: <i>ambitious, important, future-oriented</i>; conditionals: <i>if, when</i>; prepositions: <i>for, in</i>; adverbs: <i>ambitiously, carefully</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters or cloze sentences on a worksheet. Students complete them using "have to," "must," "will be + -ing," and appropriate vocabulary. (e.g., "I _______ (study) hard to pass the exam." "My most important _______ is to graduate." "If I want to _______ my _______, I _______ (prepare) carefully.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Guided Paragraph Writing):</b> Students work individually to write three to four sentences describing their *future academic goals* for high school. The teacher provides prompts like: "What do you *have to* do this year to prepare for high school?", "What subjects *must* you focus on?", "What *are your plans* for next year?", "In two years, what *will you be doing*?". Students use the target vocabulary (e.g., <i>plan, achieve, set goals, important</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check (Sentence Revision):</b> Students exchange their written paragraphs from the previous activity with a partner. Each student reads their partner's paragraph and checks for correct usage of "have to," "must," future continuous, and at least three target vocabulary words. They gently suggest revisions in writing (e.g., circle an error, write a suggestion below). Pragmatic function: Offering encouragement: "You <b>have to</b> work hard to achieve your goals."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in pairs or small groups (3 students) to collaboratively write a "Future Plans Outline" for high school or a specific group project related to future studies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative written product – A structured outline of "Our High School and Beyond Plan" or "Our Future Career Project Outline."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In groups, students discuss (Sociolinguistic: Engage in collaborative goal-setting discussions) what they expect from their future after 8th grade. They brainstorm ideas for academic paths, career interests, or skills they want to develop.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a template for their "Future Plans Outline" which includes sections like: "Our Main Goal," "What We Have to Do First," "Next Steps," "In Five Years We Will Be...".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students collaboratively write their outline, ensuring they use:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Modals of necessity: "We <b>have to</b> apply for X program." "We <b>must</b> finish our Bachillerato in Y."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Future continuous: "In three years, we <b>will be studying</b> advanced sciences."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Future time expressions and WH-questions: "What <b>are our plans</b> for the summer?" "When <b>will we start</b> researching universities?" "If we want to achieve this..."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Target vocabulary: <i>future, goals, plan, career, opportunity, achieve, prepare, set, ambitious, important, future-oriented, carefully</i>.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group writes one complete outline. They are encouraged to offer suggestions and feedback to each other during the writing process.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups submit their collaboratively written "Future Plans Outline."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation & Product Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the written plan.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of modals "have to" and "must" (at least 2-3 instances).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of future continuous (at least 1 instance).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporation of at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>future, goals, plan, career, achieve, ambitiously</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of collaboration in the writing process (e.g., equal contribution, discussion leading to shared text).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar and spelling accuracy for A2.3 level.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket individually.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or grammar point I used in my writing today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing our future plans outline?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and Teacher reflection, connection to next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Oral or written, as time permits):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did collaborating with my peers help me write about my future plans?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one concrete step I have to take next to achieve one of my future goals?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the collaborative writing task worked well? (e.g., student engagement, effective use of target language).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to further support students' ability to produce written text about future plans? (e.g., more sentence frames, focused vocabulary review).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students demonstrate the intended learning outcome for writing?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The future plans outlines or diary entries created today will serve as input for the Mediation & Integration lesson (Lesson 5), where students will consolidate their ideas, perhaps present them, or synthesize information from different group plans.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>